        <div id="power-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Amp Draw and Daily Watt-Hours, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            The spec sheet number that confuses most buyers is power draw, because there are two numbers and only one of them matters for battery sizing. The running draw is what the compressor pulls while it is actually on, commonly in the range of 3 to 5 amps at 12V for a mid-size fridge, or roughly 40 to 60 watts. The number you size a battery around is the average draw over a day, which depends on how often the compressor runs. That fraction, the duty cycle, is what changes with the weather, the set temperature and your habits.
          </p>

          <p>
            In mild weather with a full, pre-chilled fridge that stays shut most of the day, a good compressor fridge may run a quarter to a third of the time. In a hot car, at a sunny campsite or with the lid opening every half hour for drinks, it may run half the time or more. Multiply the running draw by the hours it runs and you get daily energy. A 45-watt fridge running 30 percent of the time uses about 45 x 24 x 0.30, or roughly 325 watt-hours a day. The same fridge at 50 percent uses about 540 watt-hours. That range, a few hundred watt-hours a day, is what most owners of mid-size fridges report.
          </p>

          <p>
            In amp-hours, the unit most vehicle batteries are rated in, 325 watt-hours at 12V is about 27 amp-hours a day, and 540 watt-hours is about 45 amp-hours. That is why a 100 amp-hour lithium battery, which can deliver most of its rating, runs a typical fridge for two to three days without recharging, while a 100 amp-hour lead-acid battery, which should not be discharged much past half, manages only a day or so. A car starter battery is not designed for this at all, which is where low-voltage protection comes in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Freezer use raises all of these numbers. Holding 0°F instead of 37°F means a much bigger temperature difference with the outside air, so the compressor runs far more often, and a dual-zone fridge with one side freezing uses noticeably more than the same box running as a fridge only. Ice makers use more still. If you want a freezer on a long trip, budget for a bigger battery and more solar than the fridge-only math suggests.
          </p>

