          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a thermoelectric cooler for overnight camping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thermoelectric cooler only pulls the inside about 35 to 40 degrees below the outside air. In a hot car or a sunny campsite it cannot hold food at a safe temperature, and it draws power continuously while it tries. For any trip with perishables and overnight heat, buy a compressor fridge instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the fridge from the starter battery all weekend</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A car starter battery is built for short, high bursts, not days of steady draw. Even with battery protection on its highest setting you lose cooling time, and repeated deep discharges shorten the battery life. Use a power station, a dual-battery system or a deep-cycle battery for anything beyond one night.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the pre-chill at home</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pulling a warm fridge and warm food down to temperature is the hardest work it will do all trip. Plug it into household power the night before, load it with already-cold food and drinks, and it starts the trip with a full reserve of cold and a far lower duty cycle on day one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blocking the compressor vents</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A compressor fridge has to shed the heat it removes. Packing luggage, sleeping bags or a cooler tight against the vents traps that heat, makes the compressor run longer and raises daily energy use. Leave a few inches of clearance around the vent grilles and keep the fridge out of direct sun where possible.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using thin wiring and a loose 12V socket</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Undersized cable and worn cigarette-style sockets drop voltage under load, so the fridge sees less than the battery holds and cuts out early or cycles erratically. Run heavy-gauge cable from the battery, use a locking 12V or Anderson-style connector, and check the connection before blaming the fridge.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much power does a 12V camping fridge use per day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A mid-size compressor fridge draws roughly 40 to 60 watts while the compressor runs, but it cycles on and off. In warm weather most owners see roughly 300 to 600 watt-hours a day, or about 25 to 50 amp-hours at 12V. Hot weather, frequent lid openings, freezer settings and a half-empty box push it higher; shade and pre-chilling bring it down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a 12V fridge from a portable power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is the easiest setup for most car campers. Plug the fridge into the station 12V DC socket rather than the AC outlet to avoid inverter losses. A roughly 300Wh station runs a mid-size fridge for about a day, a 1kWh station for two to three days, and adding a 200W solar panel can keep pace in good sun.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a thermoelectric cooler good enough for camping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Thermoelectric coolers only cool to about 35 to 40 degrees below the outside air, so on a 90°F day the inside sits around 50°F or warmer, too warm for meat and dairy. They also run constantly. They are fine for keeping cold drinks cool on a day drive, but a compressor fridge is the right tool for overnight trips.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big a solar panel do I need to run a camping fridge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on roughly 200 watts of panel for a mid-size compressor fridge in summer, paired with a battery or power station. A 200W panel returns roughly 500 to 700 watt-hours on a clear day, enough to cover the fridge with some margin. A 100W panel can keep up with a small fridge in good sun but falls behind quickly in cloud or shade.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 12V fridge drain my car battery overnight?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, which is why battery protection matters. Set the fridge low-voltage cutoff to its highest level when running from the starter battery, and it will shut off before the battery is too flat to start the engine. In mild weather that usually gets you through a night; in heat it may cut out earlier. A separate battery avoids the problem entirely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a dual-zone fridge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you want a freezer and a fridge running at the same time, which matters on trips longer than four or five days or when you need to freeze a catch. Dual-zone fridges are bigger, heavier, pricier and use more energy. For weekend trips, a single-zone fridge set to fridge temperature is simpler and more efficient, and it can still run as a freezer when needed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a camping fridge run on 120V household power?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most compressor fridges include or offer an AC adapter, so they run from a household outlet, campground hookup or a power station AC outlet. Use household power to pre-chill the fridge the night before a trip. At camp, prefer the 12V DC input when running from a battery, since the AC route wastes energy through two conversions.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

